#!/bin/bash
# sl7-ir-emitter-test: Stage B (measurement M2) of the SL7 IR emitter plan. It
# fires short (10 ms) pulses through the LED class sysfs, either on the ganged
# IR LED ir:flash-14 (PM8550 flash channels 1 and 4 together, --led ir, the
# stage B path, linux-sl7 7.2.8-19 or later) or, on a kernel that still has the
# four discovery LEDs (7.2.8-18 or earlier), one 12.5 mA pulse on ONE channel
# (--channel N).
#
# DISABLED BY DEFAULT. It refuses to do anything until the file
#
#     /etc/omarchy-surface-sl7/ir-stage-b-approved
#
# exists. Nothing ships that file; only the owner creates it, by hand, after reading
# research/omarchy-dragon-sl7/ir/EMITTER-PLAN.md. This tool never creates or
# removes it.
#
#   sl7-ir-emitter-test --status
#       Read-only. Shows the gate state and the ir:flash-N LEDs. No approval
#       needed, writes nothing.
#
#   sudo sl7-ir-emitter-test --i-have-read-the-plan --led ir [max_ma_per_channel=MA] [--repeat R] [--watch]
#       The ganged IR LED ir:flash-14 (channels 1 and 4, the current is split
#       equally, so the LED current is twice MA). MA is the current per channel:
#       12.5 (default), 25 or 50; 100 (the driver's hard cap) additionally asks
#       a second confirmation. Besides everything listed for --channel below
#       (approval file, IR test boot, root, terminal, R, pulse limits, fault
#       check, --watch), it
#         - types the phrase "FIRE IR <MA>MA" for every run (FIRE IR 12.5MA,
#           FIRE IR 25MA, FIRE IR 50MA, FIRE IR 100MA); 100 mA also needs
#           the second phrase "ACCEPT 100 MA PER CHANNEL"
#         - sets the driver limit ir_max_ua (/sys/module/leds_qcom_flash/
#           parameters/ir_max_ua, uA per channel) to MA after the confirmation,
#           reads it back, and sets it back to 25000 on exit
#         - writes flash_brightness = 2 x MA (uA) and flash_timeout = 10000 us
#           per pulse, each read back; the driver refuses a larger current.
#       One pulse is 10 ms: 25 / 50 / 100 / 200 mA total = 0.25 / 0.5 / 1.0 /
#       2.0 mC (Windows: about 1.11 mC per lit frame, see below).
#
#       Instrumentation (every --led ir run; linux-sl7 7.2.8-20 adds the
#       kernel side, patch 0093): the tool reads the flash register set of the
#       PMIC (offsets 0x06-0x09, 0x0c-0x0f, 0x10, 0x18, 0x3e, 0x41, 0x42, 0x45,
#       0x46, 0x49, 0x4a, 0x4d, 0x4e, 0x50-0x53 from the flash base 0xee00)
#       from the regmap debugfs (the regmap whose 0xee04/0xee05 read 18/07),
#       and logs REGDUMP lines idle before arm, with the strobe still set
#       (after the 50 ms wait, BEFORE flash_strobe=0, together with
#       flash_fault) and after the disarm. For --led ir it also sets the IR
#       sensor (vd55g subdev) to manual exposure at the control's maximum for
#       the run and restores it afterwards (--no-manual-exposure skips it; if
#       the subdev is not found the tool warns and goes on). Everything
#       printed also goes to /var/tmp/sl7-ir-<time>.txt. --pulse-delay S
#       (0..5, default 0) waits S seconds after the typed confirmation, before
#       the first pulse, so a caller can start a camera capture; it relaxes no
#       gate. --confirmed (used by sl7-ir-lab after its confirm dialog)
#       replaces the typed "FIRE IR <MA>MA" phrase for --led ir at 12.5, 25
#       and 50 mA per channel only, and then needs no terminal (the lab runs
#       the tool through pkexec); 100 mA and --channel refuse it and keep the
#       typed phrases in a terminal. Every other gate stays.
#
#   sudo sl7-ir-emitter-test --remove-snapshot
#       Fires nothing, needs no approval file (root and the IR test boot
#       only): modprobe -r leds_qcom_flash (which switches every channel off),
#       prints the "snapshot remove, after all-off" lines from the kernel log,
#       then loads the module back through the modprobe gate.
#
#   sudo sl7-ir-emitter-test --i-have-read-the-plan --channel N [--repeat R]
#       Old kernels with the discovery LEDs only. N = 1..4 (ir:flash-N,
#       led-sources = N), always 12.5 mA. R = 1..3 pulses, 1 s apart
#       (default 1). Needs all of:
#         - the approval file above (regular file, root-owned, not writable by
#           group or others, not a symlink)
#         - booted on the "linux-sl7 (IR test)" entry: sl7.ir_test=1 and panic=5
#           on /proc/cmdline, leds_qcom_flash loaded with ir_test=Y
#         - root, an interactive terminal, and the typed phrase
#           "FIRE CHANNEL N" at the confirmation prompt
#       Per pulse it writes, in this order: flash_strobe=0, flash_brightness=
#       12500, flash_timeout=10000 (each read back), then flash_strobe=1, waits
#       50 ms (the PMIC safety timer ends the pulse after 10 ms of hardware
#       time with the 0080 encoding, 0.125 mC) and writes
#       flash_strobe=0. Then it reads flash_fault: anything but
#       flash-timeout-exceeded stops the run. On any error, signal or exit every
#       ir:flash-* LED gets flash_strobe=0.
#
#   sudo sl7-ir-emitter-test --i-have-read-the-plan --channel N [--repeat R] --watch
#       Same as above (every gate, limit and the typed phrase unchanged), plus
#       an IR camera detector. Before the pulses it starts a capture from the
#       bridge loopback /dev/v4l/by-id/sl7-ir-camera (sl7-ir-bridge must be
#       active; v4l-utils and python3 are needed) into a private directory under
#       /run, waits for about 1 s of frames (auto-exposure settle), fires the
#       pulses, keeps capturing 1 s more, then analyses GREY 644x604 frames: per
#       frame mean and 99th percentile, baseline = median of the lead frames,
#       a frame is flagged when its mean or p99 exceeds the baseline by more
#       than 5 robust SDs (MAD based). Flags are aligned to the FIRE times.
#       Verdict per channel: DETECTED (every pulse spikes, 3 of 3 at R = 3),
#       WEAK (some), NONE. Only the per-frame statistics are kept (CSV next to
#       the log); no image is ever saved and the raw capture is deleted on exit.
#       Setup: white paper about 5 cm in front of the camera, angled so light
#       from beside the lens bounces back, dim room, do not move.
#
#   sudo sl7-ir-emitter-test --watch-only-test
#       A capture with no pulses (is the baseline stable?). Touches no LED, needs
#       no approval file and no typed phrase, but still needs root and the IR
#       test boot entry, and the same bridge.
#
#   sudo sl7-ir-emitter-test --i-have-read-the-plan --stage c0a|c0b|c1-sw|c1-hw|t0b [--confirmed]
#       Stage C of the plan (linux-sl7 7.2.8-21 or later, omarchy-surface-sl7 39 or
#       later). Hypothesis H2: the sensor's GPIO 1 strobe output enables the emitter
#       path, and Windows arms the PMIC for a hardware strobe. Evidence so far: a software
#       strobe gave STATUS1 0x82 (CH1 and CH4 open circuit), INT_RT_STS 0x49 (fault and
#       ramp-down, ramp-up never) and no current. The stages run in this order and each
#       refuses to run unless the one before it is recorded OK, for the running kernel, in
#       /var/lib/omarchy-surface-sl7/ir-stage-c/<stage> (the tool writes the record, with
#       the register evidence, and a time-stamped copy; --status shows them). Every stage
#       keeps all the gates of the other modes (root, IR test boot, the approval file,
#       the per-boot pulse and arm count: at most 24, a full pass is 12, the hardware
#       timer always on, the ir_max_ua cap) and is at most 25 mA per channel, enforced in
#       this file and, for the hardware strobe, in the kernel.
#       The camera must be idle: close the sl7-ir-lab live view (the lab pauses it by
#       itself for these stages). The sensor's led_mode is set BEFORE the stream starts
#       (the GPIO configuration latches at stream start) and the vd55g needs
#       illuminator=Y, which only the IR test boot sets. On every exit path: strobe off,
#       then led_mode 0, then the stream off, then exposure, blanking and auto exposure
#       back to what they were, and ir_max_ua back to 25000.
#         c0a    Hardware strobe armed (ir:flash-14, 12.5 mA per channel, about 0.7 s) with
#                the sensor NOT streaming. PASS: STATUS1 stays 0 and no ramp or fault bit
#                appears in the first 30 ms or in the samples taken while armed. Otherwise
#                STOP (recorded FAIL): the strobe input is not idle.
#         c0b    Sensor streaming with led_mode=1 set before stream-on, the PMIC NOT armed,
#                at exposure 100 lines / frame 1750 lines (about 1.3 s) and at exposure
#                6268 / frame 12600 (about 2 s), each compared with the same sensor setup
#                at led_mode=0. PASS: no brightness change (median of the frame means and
#                99th percentiles within 5 robust sd and 0.5 / 4 grey levels). Lit: STOP,
#                GPIO 1 drives the emitter on its own.
#         c1-sw  (T3) GPIO 1 strobing with the long exposure (about 100 ms high in a 200 ms
#                frame), 8 software pulses at 25 mA per channel, 10 ms, 300 to 700 ms
#                apart. Logs per pulse STATUS1, STATUS2, INT_RT_STS at +0, +1, +3 and +15
#                ms and STATUS3 at +15 ms (from the kernel snapshots), the pulse's phase
#                against the frame arrivals, and whether the frames spiked. Reports how
#                many pulses had STATUS1 == 0, the ramp-up bit (INT_RT_STS bit 4) and a
#                brightness spike. STOP on any short-circuit bit (an even STATUS1 bit),
#                STATUS2 not 0, a fault, or missing kernel evidence. Open-circuit bits
#                are a finding, not a stop.
#         c1-hw  (T4) Exposure 100 lines / frame 1750 lines, hardware strobe armed for
#                about 0.7 s at 12.5 mA per channel, then again at 25 mA per channel (two
#                sub-steps, two arms), STATUS polled back to back for 30 ms by the kernel
#                and sampled while armed, frames compared with the unarmed ones. STOP as
#                for c1-sw. The kernel disarms by itself after 1 s at the latest.
#         t0b    (optional) ir:flash-23 (channels 2 and 3), one software pulse at 12.5 mA
#                per channel, 10 ms. Expect STATUS1 0x28 (CH2 and CH3 open circuit): it
#                cross-checks how the 0x82 of channels 1 and 4 is read.
#       --confirmed (used by sl7-ir-lab after its Fire / Cancel dialog) replaces the typed
#       phrase "FIRE STAGE <NAME>" and then needs no terminal. Machine readable output:
#       STAGE_REG, STAGE_PULSE, STAGE_CMP, STAGE_PULSE_FRAMES, STAGE_ARM_FRAMES,
#       STAGE_ANALYSIS, STAGE_FRAMES csv=FILE, STAGE_RESULT stage=NAME result=OK|FAIL and
#       STAGE_SUMMARY lines. Exit 0 for OK, 5 for a recorded FAIL, 3 when the previous
#       stage is not recorded OK.
#
# The limits are constants in this file (per channel 12500, 25000, 50000 or
# 100000 uA as chosen above and never more, 10000 us, at most 3 pulses per run,
# at most 12 per boot); there is no option to raise them. The driver clamps
# independently (patch 0080: 700 mA total, 100 ms; patch 0091: ir_max_ua per
# channel with a hard cap of 100 mA per channel) and the DT limit of the ganged
# LED is 100 mA per channel / 10 ms. Raising any of them is a later, reviewed
# step.
#
# Effective pulse and charge. The PMIC safety timer field very likely counts
# (n + 1) x 10 ms, and the stock driver writes n = timeout / 10, so a stock
# "10 ms" would run about 20 ms. Patch 0080 programs n = timeout / 10 - 1 for
# IR LEDs, so the hardware pulse equals the requested 10 ms (if the field is
# really n x 10 ms the pulse is only shorter). One pulse is therefore at most
# 12.5 mA x 10 ms = 0.125 mC (one channel, discovery), about 11 % of Windows'
# 700 mA x 1.59 ms = 1.11 mC per lit frame. At 100 mA per channel the ganged
# LED draws 200 mA x 10 ms = 2.0 mC, about 1.8 times Windows' charge per frame
# (at 29 % of its current, but 6 times as long). On a kernel without the 0080
# encoding fix the pulse would be about 20 ms, twice the charge.
#
# Every action is logged to /var/log/sl7-ir-emitter-test.log and the journal
# (tag sl7-ir-emitter-test), and echoed.
#
# Run log: /var/tmp/sl7-ir-<time>.txt (everything printed), besides the log above.
#
# Exit codes: 0 done, 2 usage, 3 Stage B not approved, 4 not the IR test boot
# or hardware not as expected, 5 fault or limit, 6 sysfs write/readback failed,
# 7 not confirmed, 8 --watch problem (bridge, capture or analysis).
#
# SL7_IR_TEST_ROOT=DIR runs the whole tool against a fake tree under DIR (proc,
# sys, etc, run, var) for testing: every path below is prefixed with it and
# root, tty and ownership checks are relaxed. DIR / is refused.
set -uo pipefail

readonly CH_UA_BASE=12500
readonly CH_UA_HARD_MAX=100000
readonly IR_LIMIT_DEFAULT_UA=25000
readonly IR_NCH=2
readonly IR_LED_ID=14
readonly FIRE_TIMEOUT_US=10000
readonly MAX_REPEAT=3
readonly MAX_PULSES_PER_BOOT=12
# --stage runs: at most 25 mA per channel, at most 24 pulses or arms per boot (a
# full stage C pass is 12: c0a 1, c1-sw 8, c1-hw 2, t0b 1), shared counter.
